<section id="Ax5" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix">Appendix E: CoT with Sequential Idea Generation</h2>

<div id="Ax5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Ax5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Ax5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Experimental Setup.</span>
In Study 4, we followed <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">De Freitas et al. (2025)</a></cite> by having LLMs generate all ten ideas at once within a single session. Here, as a robustness check, we test whether CoT also improves diversity when ideas are first generated sequentially (as in Studies 1–3) and then revised using a CoT-style prompt following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">De Freitas et al. (2025)</a></cite>. We generated two conditions: <span id="Ax5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">LLM CoT</span> and <span id="Ax5.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">LLM Persona CoT</span>, and compared them to the corresponding baseline conditions from previous studies.</p>
</div>
<div id="Ax5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Ax5.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Ax5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">LLM Conditions.</span> We apply CoT revisions to two different sources of base ideas.</p>
</div>
<div id="Ax5.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Ax5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">(1) LLM CoT Revision. For each simulated LLM participant (<math id="Ax5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=99" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>99</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=99</annotation></semantics></math>) from Study 1, we take their original set of ten sequentially generated ideas and ask the model to revise them using a CoT-style prompt. The ten ideas are directly input into the revision prompt.</p>
</div>
